        <div id="battery-sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Battery Sizes Explained: 18650, 21700, 26650 and Built-In
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rechargeable flashlights mostly run on cylindrical lithium-ion cells named for their size: an 18650 is 18 mm wide and 65 mm long, a 21700 is 21 by 70, and a 26650 is 26 by 65. Bigger cells hold more energy. A typical 18650 holds around 3,000 to 3,500 mAh, a 21700 around 4,000 to 5,000 mAh, and a 26650 in a similar range to a large 21700 but in a fatter body. At a nominal 3.6 volts, a 5,000 mAh 21700 stores about 18 watt-hours, which is the same energy as a small phone power bank.
          </p>

          <p>
            That stored energy is what you are really buying. On a medium setting of a couple of hundred lumens, a 21700 light can run for many hours; the manufacturer's runtime chart will give the figure for each mode. On turbo, the same battery empties in a fraction of the time, which is why every light on this list steps down from turbo on its own. Read the runtime chart before the lumen headline: it tells you how long the light lasts at the brightness you will actually use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in batteries are the trade-off on slim lights like the Nitecore EDC27 and budget lights like the Anker LC40. They make the light thinner, cheaper or both, but when the cell wears out after a few hundred cycles the light is done. A removable 18650 or 21700 can be replaced in seconds, and you can keep a charged spare in the kit, which doubles your runtime with no extra charging during an outage.
          </p>

