        <div id="lumens-vs-runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Lumens vs Runtime: Reading the Spec Sheet Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flashlight makers rate output and runtime under the ANSI FL1 standard, and the standard is more forgiving than most buyers realise. The peak lumen figure is measured shortly after switching on with a fresh cell. Runtime is measured until output falls to 10 percent of the starting value. So a light can be listed at a big lumen number and a long runtime, but spend most of that runtime at a level far below the headline.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to ignore turbo when comparing lights and look at the medium mode instead. Somewhere between 100 and 400 lumens is what you use indoors to walk around, cook by and check on kids. A good 21700 light can sustain that kind of output for hours. Turbo is for looking across a yard or finding a pet outside, and you will use it for seconds at a time.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy on the medium-mode runtime, not the turbo lumens.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Turbo is a burst. The setting you live with in an outage is medium, and the runtime chart for that mode is the honest comparison between lights.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Heat is why turbo steps down. A small aluminium body can only shed so much heat, and LEDs and cells both suffer when hot. Every reputable maker builds in thermal or timed step-down, and a light that claims to hold 4,000 lumens for an hour from a handheld body is not telling the truth. The ThruNite TC20 V2 makes the most light here, and it too drops to a lower sustained level after a short burst.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beam shape matters as much as lumen count. A light with a deep reflector throws a tight beam far down a road; a shallow reflector or TIR optic floods a wide area close up. For indoor outage use, flood is more useful. For a yard, a driveway or checking a roof line, throw helps. The zoom head on the Anker LC40 is a cheap way to get both from one light.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. USB-C, Magnetic Cables and Charging During an Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            USB-C charging is the single most practical feature to look for. Every light on this list charges over USB-C except the Olight, which uses a magnetic tail cable. USB-C means the light charges from whatever you are already using to keep phones alive: a wall charger while the power is on, then a power bank, a <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">portable charger</a>, a car outlet or a power station once it is off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charging time depends on the cell and the light's charging circuit, and a big 21700 or 26650 cell commonly takes a few hours from empty. That is fine in normal use but worth planning around in an outage. Charge lights first thing in the morning from your power bank or station, not at night when you need them, and rotate two lights so one is always charging while the other is in use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Magnetic charging, as on the Olight Warrior 3S, has real advantages. It is quick to attach, works by feel and leaves the light sealed against water. The weakness is that the cable is proprietary. If it is lost or left in a car, there is no borrowing a phone cable instead. Anyone who buys an Olight should buy a spare charging cable and keep it with the outage supplies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some larger lights can also act as a small power bank, pushing charge out to a phone. It is a nice feature but a poor trade in an outage, because every watt-hour you move from the flashlight to the phone is light you no longer have. A dedicated power bank or <a href="/best-solar-lantern/">solar lantern</a> with a USB output is a better phone charger, and leaves the flashlight's battery for light.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Building a Flashlight Kit for a Power Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            A flashlight is the first thing anyone reaches for when the power goes out, and the most common failure is simple: nobody can find one, or the one they find is dead. The fix is more lights in more places, charged on a routine. A good household kit has one main light, a carry light per adult and a cheap light in every room people sleep in.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the main light, pick something with a large removable cell and, ideally, a backup battery type. The Streamlight ProTac HL-X USB is our pick for this job because it keeps working on CR123A primaries after the rechargeable cell is flat. The Fenix PD36R V2.0 is the alternative if you want a bigger rechargeable battery and plan to recharge from a power bank or station.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flashlights are the wrong tool for lighting a room. A flashlight pointed at a ceiling works for an hour, but for an evening of cooking, reading and playing cards you want a lantern, which spreads light evenly and runs far longer at room-level output. Pair your flashlights with a <a href="/best-battery-powered-lantern/">battery lantern</a> or two, and keep the flashlights for moving around, going outside and checking equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then plan the charging. A mid-size power bank holds several times the energy of a 21700 cell, so one power bank can recharge every flashlight in the house more than once. For outages that run for days, a power station or a <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator</a> covers recharging easily, and an <a href="/best-emergency-radio/">emergency radio</a> with a hand crank keeps you informed when everything else is flat.
          </p>

