        <div id="battery-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Battery Care, Storage and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion cells last longest when they are not stored full and hot or empty for months. For a light that lives in an emergency kit, the simplest routine is to charge it fully at the start of each storm season, use it now and then, and top it up every few months. A light that sits for a year untouched may still work, but it will not have the runtime you expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Removable cells need a little care. Use the cell the maker supplies or a reputable replacement of the same type, and check whether the light needs a button-top or flat-top cell, and whether it uses a cell with a built-in charging port. Cheap unbranded cells with inflated capacity claims are common and worth avoiding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not carry loose lithium-ion cells in a pocket or a bag with keys or coins. A short circuit across the terminals can heat a cell fast. Spare cells should travel in a plastic case. The same caution applies to CR123A primaries, which store a lot of energy in a small package.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat is the enemy of every battery, so the glove box in midsummer is a poor home for a flashlight. If you keep one in the car, accept that its battery will age faster and check it more often. A budget light like the LC40 is a sensible choice for the car because it costs little to replace when the heat finally kills the cell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which One Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want one rechargeable flashlight and do not want to think about it, buy the Fenix PD36R V2.0. It has the large 21700 cell, USB-C charging, a sensible interface and a battery you can replace, and it is bright enough for every household job.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your main worry is a long outage, buy the Streamlight ProTac HL-X USB and a pack of CR123A cells. It is the one light here that keeps working when recharging is impossible, which is exactly the scenario an emergency light is for.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the most light per dollar for the yard and driveway, the ThruNite TC20 V2 is the answer. If you want something you will actually carry every day, the Nitecore EDC27 is the thinnest bright light here. If you want the best-built tactical light and will keep track of its cable, the Olight Warrior 3S is excellent.
          </p>

          <p>
            And whatever you buy as the main light, add a couple of Anker Bolder LC40s for the bedrooms and the car. Most outage stories that end with a stubbed toe or a fall on the stairs start with a flashlight that was in a different room.
          </p>
