          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the lumen number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Peak lumens last seconds before the light steps down. Compare lights on battery size and the runtime at medium output, which is the mode you will use for hours. A bigger battery at 1,700 lumens beats a small one claiming 4,000 for almost every household job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Owning one flashlight for the whole house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common outage problem is not a weak light, it is no light in the room you are standing in. Put a cheap rechargeable light in every bedroom, the kitchen and the car, and keep the good one in the main kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on a proprietary charging cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Magnetic cables are convenient until one goes missing. If your light uses one, buy a spare and store it with the outage supplies. Otherwise prefer USB-C, which charges from any phone cable and power bank in the house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it and forgetting it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lithium-ion light left untouched for a year will not deliver its rated runtime when you need it. Charge every flashlight at the start of storm season and top it up every few months. It takes minutes and avoids a dead light in the dark.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a flashlight as room lighting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A flashlight aimed at the ceiling drains fast and lights a room poorly. For cooking, reading and evenings in an outage, use a lantern and save the flashlight's battery for moving around, going outside and checking equipment.
              </p>
