        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best rechargeable flashlight overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households the Fenix PD36R V2.0 is the best balance. It pairs a 5,000 mAh 21700 cell with USB-C charging, IP68 water resistance and a battery you can replace when it ages. It is bright enough for every indoor and outdoor job and simple enough for anyone in the house to use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are rechargeable flashlights good for power outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as you can recharge them. A large 21700 light runs for hours at medium output, and a power bank or power station can refill it several times. For long outages, a light that also takes CR123A primary cells, like the Streamlight ProTac HL-X USB, keeps working when recharging is not possible.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a rechargeable flashlight battery last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Runtime per charge depends on the mode; check the maker's runtime chart for medium output, not turbo. Over the long term, a lithium-ion cell typically lasts several hundred charge cycles before capacity drops noticeably. A light with a removable cell can be renewed with a new battery; a built-in battery means replacing the light.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 1,000 lumens bright enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For nearly everything a household does, yes. Walking around indoors needs 50 to 150 lumens and working at a panel or cooking needs a few hundred. A thousand lumens lights a yard well. Higher figures are useful outdoors at distance, but they are short bursts and drain the battery quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between an 18650 and a 21700 battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are both lithium-ion cells named for their size. A 21700 is wider and longer, so it holds more energy, typically 4,000 to 5,000 mAh against 3,000 to 3,500 for an 18650. In a flashlight that means longer runtime at the same brightness, at the cost of a slightly larger body.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge a rechargeable flashlight from a power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Any light with a USB-C port charges from a power bank, a car USB outlet or a portable power station, which is exactly how you keep it running during an outage. Lights with a proprietary magnetic cable can do the same, but only with that specific cable, so keep a spare.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I keep spare batteries for a rechargeable flashlight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For lights with removable cells, a charged spare is the simplest way to double your runtime without charging. Store spares in a plastic case away from keys and coins. If your light also accepts CR123A primaries, a sealed pack of those is the best long-term emergency backup because they store for years.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
